# What git may translate, decided here rather than left to a per-clone guess.
#
# text=auto does not fix the working-tree ending: that still follows each
# clone's core.autocrlf and core.eol. What it fixes is which files are eligible
# for translation at all, and that is the part that was a guess. This machine
# has core.autocrlf=true and the CI runners do not, so every file has been
# round-tripping through a setting recorded nowhere, with git deciding text from
# binary by a heuristic over the first 8000 bytes. The index is normalised to LF
# either way; what this line settles is that the decision is the same in every
# clone.
* text=auto

# web/pool.bin is the load-bearing line. It is 1,000,000 bytes that
# tests/test_tools_output.py compares byte for byte against a fresh export, and
# tests/test_pool.py and web/live.js both decode. It survives core.autocrlf
# today only because git's heuristic found NUL bytes early enough to call it
# binary, which is a safe inference for 200,000 packed boards and stops being
# one the moment someone exports a small debug pool. CRLF translation on it
# would shift every offset past the first 0x0A and the decoders would read
# nonsense.
*.bin binary

# Deliberately absent: any eol=crlf rule on *.txt.
#
# docs/ holds four captured tool transcripts as .txt, and the nightly
# regenerates three of them and prints the byte diff against the committed
# copies. WEIGHTED_MARGINALS.txt is the fourth and is left out deliberately: it
# reports its own wall-clock seconds, so it cannot be compared byte for byte,
# and no published number depends on it.
#
# Forcing CRLF here would make that diff report every line of all three
# regenerated transcripts as changed, every night, while the job stayed green,
# because the gate is the collected values rather than the bytes. The
# diagnostic would be destroyed and nothing would say so, which is the exact
# failure shape this repository keeps finding.
